<title>POLAND: POLISH H1 C/A DEFICIT SOARS TO $2.697 BLN.</title>
<headline>POLISH H1 C/A DEFICIT SOARS TO $2.697 BLN.</headline>
<dateline>WARSAW 1997-08-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>Poland had a current account deficit of $287 million in June, against a $135 million gap in May, bringing the shortfall in the first six months of 1997 to $2.697 billion, the National Bank of Poland said on Tuesday.</p>
<p>In the first six months of 1996 the C/A gap amounted to $272 million, the central bank said.  </p>
<p> POLISH CURRENT ACCOUNT	   JUNE	  MAY	JUNE 96</p>
<p> current account million USD	  -287	 -135	  -65</p>
<p> merchandise trade balance	-898	 -824	 -535</p>
<p> exports			     2,296	2,035	1.876</p>
<p> imports			     3,194	2,859	2.411</p>
<p> unclassified flows balance	474	  552	  474</p>
<p>@</p>
<p>					 JAN-JUNE 1997  JAN-JUNE 1996</p>
<p> current account million USD	  -2,697	    -272</p>
<p> merchandise trade balance	-5,724	  -3,146</p>
<p> exports				12,458	  11,411</p>
<p> imports				18,182	  14,557</p>
<p> unclassified flows balance	2,778	   3,497</p>
<p>Note. From January 1996 the central bank introduced a new category, &quot;unclassified flows&quot; which encompasses mainly cross-border, unregistered trade flows.</p>
<p>-- Warsaw Newsroom +48 22 653 9700</p>
